{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|essart}} from {{der|en|VL.|exsartum}}.<ref>{{R:fr:TLFi|essart}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈsɑː(ɹ)t/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-assart.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)t|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[forest|Forest]] [[land]] [[cleared]] for [[agriculture]].
#: {{syn|en|riding#Noun|royd#Noun<qq:dialectal>|thwaite#Noun}}
#: {{cot|en|glade#Noun|meadow#Noun|clearcut#Noun}}
# {{lb|en|legal|obsolete}} The act or offence of grubbing up trees and bushes, and thus destroying the [[thicket]]s or [[covert]]s of a [[forest]].
#: {{hyper|en|slashing#Noun}}
#: {{cot|en|clearcut#Noun}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1607|author={{w|John Cowell (jurist)|John Cowell}}|title=The Interpreter: or Booke Containing the Signification of Words|location=Cambridge|publisher=John Legate|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A19476.0001.001
|passage={{...}} an '''''assart''''' of the Forest, is the greatest offence or trespasse of all other, that can be done in the forest, to vert or venison, containing in it as much as waste or more. For whereas the waste of the Forest, is but the felling and cutting downe of the couerts, which may grow againe in time: an '''''assart''''', is a plucking them vp {{...}}}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To clear forest land for [[agriculture]]; remove [[stump|stumps]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1661|author={{w|Thomas Blount (lexicographer)|Thomas Blount}}|title=Glossographia|location=London|publisher=George Sawbridge|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A28464.0001.001
|passage={{...}} if a man sue out a Licence to '''''assart''''' his grounds in the Forest, and to make it several for Tillage, then it is no offence.}}
#* '''1775''', {{w|John Ash (divine)|John Ash}}, ''The New and Complete Dictionary of the English Language'', London: Edward & Charles Dilly, Volume I,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/008590346]</sup>
#*: '''ASSART''' ''v.t.'' {{...}} To clear away wood.

